**Clarify.** What happens when the warehouse is full: block, drop, or fail? Does a producer have to pick a specific slot or any free one? Is there a consumer removing cars? How many cars does each producer make?

**Design.** Model the warehouse as an array of slots. Protect each slot with its own `ReentrantLock` (fine-grained locking, so four producers rarely contend on the same slot). A producer scans slots, takes the lock only if it is free using `tryLock()`, checks the slot is empty, places the car and unlocks in `finally`.

    class Warehouse {
        private final Car[] slots;
        private final ReentrantLock[] locks;
        private final Semaphore free;                  // counts empty slots
        Warehouse(int n) {
            slots = new Car[n];
            locks = new ReentrantLock[n];
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) locks[i] = new ReentrantLock();
            free = new Semaphore(n);
        }
        void place(Car c) throws InterruptedException {
            free.acquire();                            // blocks when full
            while (true) {
                for (int i = 0; i < slots.length; i++) {
                    if (locks[i].tryLock()) {
                        try { if (slots[i] == null) { slots[i] = c; return; } }
                        finally { locks[i].unlock(); }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    class Producer implements Runnable { /* loop: create car, warehouse.place(car) */ }
    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) pool.submit(new Producer(w, carsEach));
    pool.shutdown();

The semaphore guarantees a free slot exists, so the scan terminates; it also stops threads from spinning when the warehouse is full. If a consumer removes a car it must null the slot under that slot's lock and call `free.release()`.

**Alternatives.** `AtomicReferenceArray` with `compareAndSet(i, null, car)` is lock-free. A `BlockingQueue` of free slot indices (take an index, place, done) is simplest of all. A single `synchronized` method is correct but serialises everything.

**Pitfalls:** always unlock in `finally`, never hold two slot locks at once (deadlock risk), and call `shutdown()` and `awaitTermination`. `submit` accepts both `Runnable` and `Callable`.
